WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.760 --> 00:00:03.962
Hello! Welcome back to Module 2.

2
00:00:03.962 --> 00:00:08.367
In this video, we will continue
exploring the National Data tab,

3
00:00:08.367 --> 00:00:11.740
specifically the Community
Participation section.

4
00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:15.309
On the top left corner of the
page, click on National Data.

5
00:00:15.730 --> 00:00:19.420
Under Category, click on
Community Participation.

6
00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:24.300
There are several topics within
community participation,

7
00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:30.239
including health insurance, transportation,
education, as well as technology access.

8
00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:35.031
This section is a little bit different
than what we've seen before.

9
00:00:35.031 --> 00:00:39.486
Here, we have two maps. The one on the
left shows people with disabilities,

10
00:00:39.486 --> 00:00:43.170
and the one on the right shows
us people without disabilities.

11
00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:46.110
Let's start by looking at
health insurance data.

12
00:00:46.810 --> 00:00:52.259
This first page shows us people
ages 19 to 64 who are uninsured,

13
00:00:52.259 --> 00:00:56.260
meaning they do not have
any form of insurance.

14
00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:59.715
This is not considered a positive outcome.

15
00:00:59.715 --> 00:01:04.739
We want to see states that have lower
percentages of uninsured people.

16
00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:09.519
This is because we want to see
higher rates of insured people.

17
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:14.546
Under the map, there is a key that
tells us exactly what each color means.

18
00:01:14.546 --> 00:01:17.433
In this map, the lighter
colors mean that there

19
00:01:17.433 --> 00:01:22.041
are more uninsured people who live in
that state. This is not a good thing.

20
00:01:22.041 --> 00:01:24.068
We want to see the darker colors,

21
00:01:24.068 --> 00:01:27.570
because this means that there
are fewer uninsured people.

22
00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:33.909
Generally, looking at this map, I can
see that people without disabilities

23
00:01:33.909 --> 00:01:38.900
experience higher rates of being
uninsured. You may wonder, why is that?

24
00:01:39.130 --> 00:01:42.749
Let's look at the data table to
find more specific information.

25
00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:46.502
We've seen this data table before.

26
00:01:46.502 --> 00:01:50.547
We know on the left-hand side,
it's going to show us the state,

27
00:01:50.547 --> 00:01:52.729
territories, or the United States.

28
00:01:52.729 --> 00:01:56.966
Next to this, it shows us the number
and percentage of people with

29
00:01:56.966 --> 00:02:01.780
disabilities between the ages of 19 to
64 who do not have health insurance.

30
00:02:01.870 --> 00:02:06.705
Next to this, it shows us the same thing,
but for people without disabilities.

31
00:02:06.705 --> 00:02:09.680
So let's take a look at
California, for example.

32
00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:14.222
This shows us that 7.1% of
people with disabilities

33
00:02:14.222 --> 00:02:18.750
between the ages of 19 to 64
do not have health insurance.

34
00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:24.561
To the right of this, it shows us that
9.5% of people without disabilities

35
00:02:24.561 --> 00:02:28.751
between the ages of 19 to 64
don't have health insurance.

36
00:02:28.751 --> 00:02:32.795
So we can see here that people
without disabilities are

37
00:02:32.795 --> 00:02:37.059
more likely to be uninsured
than people with disabilities.

38
00:02:38.450 --> 00:02:42.660
Let's scroll to the top and look at the
different types of health insurance.

39
00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:47.529
There are two main types of
health insurance categories.

40
00:02:47.529 --> 00:02:50.709
Generally, there's public
or private insurance.

41
00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:53.864
Private insurance is usually
provided by your employer,

42
00:02:53.864 --> 00:02:56.520
and it's insurances like
Blue Cross Blue Shield.

43
00:02:56.520 --> 00:02:58.900
You can explore this
data on your own time.

44
00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:04.038
Public health insurance includes Medicare,
which is for people ages 65 and older,

45
00:03:04.038 --> 00:03:07.576
and some people with disabilities,
and then there's Medicaid,

46
00:03:07.576 --> 00:03:10.650
which is for people with low
incomes or a disability.

47
00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:15.361
Looking at these two maps, I
notice a distinct difference.

48
00:03:15.361 --> 00:03:19.288
I see that many people with
disabilities between the

49
00:03:19.288 --> 00:03:22.989
ages of 19 to 64 are insured
by public insurance.

50
00:03:23.370 --> 00:03:28.070
Whereas fewer people without
disabilities have public insurance.

51
00:03:29.320 --> 00:03:33.250
To find more specific information, you
can scroll down to the data table.

52
00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:37.140
I also want to point out one
feature that might be helpful.

53
00:03:37.330 --> 00:03:40.399
On the data table, there are these arrows.

54
00:03:40.660 --> 00:03:42.158
When you click on them,

55
00:03:42.158 --> 00:03:47.368
it sorts the data either from the lowest
percentages to the highest percentages.

56
00:03:47.368 --> 00:03:51.797
So this can show you easily which
states have the highest percentage

57
00:03:51.797 --> 00:03:56.030
of people with public insurance
in that age, and then the lowest.

58
00:03:57.470 --> 00:03:59.093
Let's scroll back to the top.

59
00:03:59.093 --> 00:04:03.180
Let's look at the types of transportation
that people use to get to work.

60
00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:08.259
Let's look at commute to
work by public transit.

61
00:04:08.950 --> 00:04:12.915
Public transportation includes
the bus, the subway, and trains.

62
00:04:12.915 --> 00:04:16.566
So we have two maps comparing
people with disabilities and

63
00:04:16.566 --> 00:04:20.279
without disabilities who commute
to work by public transit.

64
00:04:20.589 --> 00:04:23.534
We can see that more
people with disabilities

65
00:04:23.534 --> 00:04:28.769
commute to work by public transportation,
as represented by these darker colors.

66
00:04:28.900 --> 00:04:33.960
This may be because of the nature of the
disability, or this may be by choice.

67
00:04:35.140 --> 00:04:37.839
Let's scroll back up
and look at education.

68
00:04:39.220 --> 00:04:45.204
I want to specifically focus on no high
school diploma, GED equivalent, ages 25+.

69
00:04:45.204 --> 00:04:49.120
Please explore the other
categories on your own time.

70
00:04:50.990 --> 00:04:54.086
So this map shows us people
who did not complete

71
00:04:54.086 --> 00:04:56.988
their high school degree
or a GED equivalent.

72
00:04:56.988 --> 00:05:01.051
In these maps, the lighter colors
represent a negative outcome,

73
00:05:01.051 --> 00:05:05.629
meaning there are more people who did
not receive a high school degree.

74
00:05:05.629 --> 00:05:08.209
Therefore, we want to see darker colors.

75
00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:13.299
We can see pretty clearly that on the
left-hand side, that in most states,

76
00:05:13.299 --> 00:05:17.470
many people with disabilities did
not get a high school degree.

77
00:05:17.800 --> 00:05:21.280
Let's look at the data table to
find more specific information.

78
00:05:23.220 --> 00:05:25.569
Let's look at California, for example.

79
00:05:25.860 --> 00:05:30.458
In California, 23% of people
with disabilities over the age

80
00:05:30.458 --> 00:05:34.979
of 25 did not receive a high
school diploma or equivalent.

81
00:05:35.240 --> 00:05:39.099
Comparatively, only 13.8% of
people without disabilities

82
00:05:39.099 --> 00:05:42.681
in these ages did not receive
a high school diploma.

83
00:05:42.681 --> 00:05:47.987
So many more people with disabilities
don't receive this high school diploma,

84
00:05:47.987 --> 00:05:53.499
comparatively to people without disabilities.
That's a pretty interesting point.

85
00:05:53.940 --> 00:05:56.239
Why do you think there's
such a large difference?

86
00:05:57.830 --> 00:05:59.460
Let's scroll to the top.

87
00:05:59.650 --> 00:06:02.550
And explore technology access.

88
00:06:02.840 --> 00:06:04.510
Let's look at computer.

89
00:06:08.850 --> 00:06:11.027
In this section, there's only one map.

90
00:06:11.027 --> 00:06:15.610
It shows the percentage of people with
disabilities who have access to a laptop.

91
00:06:16.060 --> 00:06:17.903
Generally, looking at the table,

92
00:06:17.903 --> 00:06:22.050
I can see that more people without
disabilities have access to a laptop.

93
00:06:24.580 --> 00:06:29.082
Alright, so that concludes the
information on community participation.

94
00:06:29.082 --> 00:06:31.783
We took a look at data on health insurance

95
00:06:31.783 --> 00:06:36.284
and the different types of people
may have, education levels obtained,

96
00:06:36.284 --> 00:06:40.979
the types of transportation people
take to work, the education levels the

97
00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:43.601
And as well as technology access.

98
00:06:43.601 --> 00:06:48.120
In the next video, we will look at
work and economic data. Thank you!
